.TH BTOON 1 "November 2026" "btoon 0.1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
btoon \- convert between JSON and the BTOON binary format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btoon encode
.RI [ IN.json | \- ]
.RI [ OUT.btoon | \- ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B btoon decode
.RI [ IN.btoon | \- ]
.RI [ OUT.json | \- ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B btoon info
.RI [ IN.btoon | \- ]
.br
.B btoon
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
BTOON is MessagePack with a columnar encoding for arrays of uniform objects, extra types
(timestamps, big integers, float vectors) and optional compression/HMAC envelopes.
.B btoon
converts JSON documents to canonical BTOON and back, and describes BTOON files.
A missing or \fB\-\fR path means standard input/output.
The mapping is lossless: decoding a canonical BTOON document to JSON and encoding the
JSON again gives the same bytes, for every BTOON type.
.SH JSON MAPPING
JSON null, booleans, strings, arrays and objects map directly. Integers become BTOON
integers, and integer literals outside the 64-bit range become big integers (literals
beyond about 1e308 are rejected; write those as \fB$bigint\fR). Numbers with a fraction
or an exponent become 64-bit floats.
.PP
Values JSON can't represent are written as single-key tagged objects, which
\fBencode\fR recognizes again:
.TP
\fB{"$binary": "\fIbase64\fB"}\fR
Binary data. The base64 is decoded strictly (standard or URL-safe alphabet, padding
optional).
.TP
\fB{"$timestamp": "2024-01-01T00:00:00.5Z"}\fR
A timestamp in RFC 3339 form: \fIYYYY\fB\-\fIMM\fB\-\fIDD\fBT\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fB:\fIss\fR,
an optional fraction of 1 to 9 digits, then \fBZ\fR (no offset) or
\fB+\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR / \fB\-\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR (an offset of at most 18 hours, which is
kept). The date must exist, and leap seconds and trailing text are rejected. Years
outside 0000\(en9999 are written with a sign and at least six digits, for example
\fB+010000\fR or \fB\-000001\fR.
.TP
\fB{"$date": \fImilliseconds\fB}\fR
A date: an integer count of milliseconds since the Unix epoch.
.TP
\fB{"$datetime": "\fInanoseconds\fB"}\fR
A datetime: nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, as a decimal string.
.TP
\fB{"$bigint": "\fIdecimal\fB"}\fR
A big integer. Values longer than 40,000 bytes (about 96,000 digits) are written in hex
instead, as \fB"0x\fR...\fB"\fR or \fB"\-0x\fR...\fB"\fR, which keeps conversion fast.
Both forms are accepted. Decimal input is limited to 100,000 digits; use hex for more.
.TP
\fB{"$float": "NaN"}\fR, \fB"Infinity"\fR, \fB"\-Infinity"\fR
Floats that JSON numbers can't hold. No other strings are accepted.
.TP
\fB{"$f32": [\fR...\fB]}\fR, \fB{"$f64": [\fR...\fB]}\fR
Float32 and float64 vectors, as numbers and \fB$float\fR objects. A vector that holds a
NaN other than the default quiet NaN is written as base64 of its little-endian payload,
\fB{"$f32": "\fIbase64\fB"}\fR, so the NaN bits survive. Both forms are accepted.
.TP
\fB{"$ext": [\fItype\fB, "\fIbase64\fB"]}\fR
An extension. The type is an integer from \-128 to 127, other than the ids BTOON defines
itself (\-1 to \-6 and \-10).
.TP
\fB{"$map": {\fR...\fB}}\fR
A map that would otherwise read as a tag. Every map whose only key starts with \fB$\fR
is written this way, so that a map such as \fB{"$binary": "x"}\fR round-trips.
.PP
A single-key object whose key is one of these tags is always that tag, and malformed
content is an error. Other keys that start with \fB$\fR are ordinary keys. Values nest
at most 128 levels deep, as in BTOON itself (tag objects don't count); deeper input fails
with \fBmax_depth\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-compress [ =\fIALGO\fR ]
Wrap the output in a compression envelope.
.I ALGO
is zlib (the default), lz4, zstd, brotli or snappy, depending on the build. Decoding
detects envelopes automatically.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tabular
Disable the columnar encoding. The output is then plain canonical MessagePack.
.TP
.BI \-\-hmac\-key\-file " FILE"
When encoding, sign with HMAC-SHA256 using the bytes of
.IR FILE .
When decoding, require and verify a signature made with that key. An empty file is an
error, because an empty key would mean no signature at all.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Write single-line JSON when decoding.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tags
Don't interpret \fB$\fR-tagged objects when encoding.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 on a conversion or I/O error, including an output that can't be written
(a full disk, a closed standard output); the message includes the BTOON error code, for
example \fBtruncated\fR, \fBmax_depth\fR or \fBauthentication_failed\fR. 2 on a usage
error, such as an unknown option or an empty key file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
btoon encode data.json data.btoon \-\-compress
btoon decode data.btoon \- | jq .
btoon info data.btoon
cat data.json | btoon encode \- \- | btoon decode \- \- \-\-compact
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR btoon\-convert " and " btoon\-schema
(run them with \fB\-\-help\fR).
The mapping is implemented in tools/json_mapping.h. The format specification is at
https://github.com/BTOON-project/btoon-core/blob/main/docs/SPEC.md
